@using BulkyBook.Models

@* (46) Now we add the model to bind the info added in the view to the controller 
    Model is more like an object which binds its properties with the view.
*@
@model Category




@* (45) Create view created to add categories
The code snippet for this view is copied from "Course Content\Snippets\Section 3 - Category List\5 Category Create.txt"
*@

<div class="container-fluid py-3">
    <a href="#" class="text-decoration-none text-secondary small fw-medium d-inline-flex align-items-center gap-1 mb-3">
        <i class="bi bi-arrow-left"></i> Back to categories
    </a>
    <div class="card border shadow-sm overflow-hidden">
        <div class="card-header text-white py-3 px-4" style="background: linear-gradient(to right, #065f46, #282828, #191414);">
            <h5 class="mb-0 fw-bold"><i class="bi bi-plus-lg me-2"></i>Create Category</h5>
            <p class="mb-0 small" style="color: #1DB954;">Add a new category for your products</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body p-4">
            @* (50) Once we fill all inputs, we need to submit this form. For that we add method="post" attribute in the form 
                This form will be posted in the category controller. There we have an HTTPPost CreatePost method which is hit when you submit this form.
            *@
            <form method="post">
                @* (60) To add validations we have something called as asp-validation-summary This will display all the error meesages on top as "All" is selected
                    All: gives all the errors on the page, along with the data annotations
                    ModelOnly: returns the errors if they are linked with the model (In this case it is Category).
                *@
              @*   <div asp-validation-summary="All" class="text-danger mb-3"></div> *@
                <div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly" class="text-danger mb-3"></div>
                @* (64) Earlier we set the asp-validation-summary to "All"
                    which will display all the errors on the page, along with the data annotations.
                    However, if we want to display the validations on each field we will set it to ModelOnly 
                    and add new tag helper asp-validation-for
                    Same for DisplayOrder. This will displaydata annotations error messages.
                    If something is not related to data annotations and is linked with the model itself
                    (for eg duplicate category name), that will be
                    displayed using asp-validation-summary *@
                <div class="mb-3">
                  @*   <label asp-for="Name" class="form-label fw-semibold small">Category Name</label> *@
                  @* (118) Removig Category Name from here since we have asp-for, it would directly display the name given in Display attribute *@
                    <label asp-for="Name" class="form-label fw-semibold small"></label>
                    @* (47) The bind is done in cshtml using command "asp-for"
                        asp-for is a tag helper attribute in ASP.NET Core that binds an HTML element to a model property
                        The Name here in asp-for is the property under Category class model.
                    *@
                    <input type="text" name="Name" asp-for="Name" class="form-control" placeholder="Enter category name" />
                    @* (48) OR you can aslo write @Model.Name but that is not required *@
               @*      <input type="text" name="Name" asp-for="@Model.Name" class="form-control" placeholder="Enter category name" /> *@

                    <span asp-validation-for="Name" class="text-danger small"></span>
                </div>
                <div class="mb-3">
                    @* (66)  adding asp-for here and same for name. This is added because when we click on the Label, the input box is highlighted automatically*@
                    <label asp-for="DisplayOrder" class="form-label fw-semibold small">Display Order</label>
@*                     (49) Same we will add for Display order
 *@                    <input type="number" name="DisplayOrder" asp-for="DisplayOrder" class="form-control" placeholder="Enter display order" />
                    <span asp-validation-for="DisplayOrder" class="text-danger small"></span>
                </div>
                <div class="d-flex gap-2 mt-4">
                    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-accent fw-semibold">Create Category</button>
                    @* (67) Adding Cancel button to go back the Category -> Index page *@
                    <a asp-controller="Category" asp-action="Index" class="btn btn-light fw-medium">Cancel</a>
                </div>
            </form>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@* (65) Adding client side validations
This is in java script and is done using jquery validation plugin. This is already added in the _Layout.cshtml file.
The scripts are referenced in the _ValidationScriptsPartial.cshtml file.
So the data annotations are directly done on client side and the break point CreatePOST POST method of CategoryController is not hit.
It is hit only when the client side/ data annotations validations are valid and we want to then call the HOST method
// to then check the server side validation done inside the POST method or on Server side.
*@

@section Scripts
{
    <partial name="_ValidationScriptsPartial" />
}
